@node The problem
@chapter The problem

Elpaca installs a package by cloning its source@comma{} byte-compiling the elisp@comma{} and
generating autoloads. For most packages that is everything needed. For roughly
16% of GNU/NonGNU ELPA packages (and a smaller slice of MELPA packages)@comma{} there
are additional build steps that upstream @emph{does} run — on the ELPA/MELPA build
server — but that Elpaca does not replay locally.

Concrete examples of what gets lost:

@itemize
@item
AUC@TeX{} ships @samp{latex/preview.sty} and friends only after @samp{make elpa} runs.
Without that step@comma{} the @samp{preview-latex} feature fails with @samp{File 'preview.sty'
  not found.} at @LaTeX{} time.
@item
ESS@comma{} Eat@comma{} Evil@comma{} Bongo and dozens of others ship Info manuals that only exist
after @samp{makeinfo} runs — in some cases with @samp{cd} into a @samp{doc/} subdirectory
first so @samp{@@include} paths resolve@comma{} in others with a preceding @samp{make} to
generate a @samp{version.texi}.
@item
A handful of packages (Hyperbole@comma{} Tramp@comma{} flymake-collection@comma{} etc.) need a
@samp{make} target that produces a @samp{.el} file at runtime.
@end itemize

@samp{package.el} sidesteps all of this by downloading pre-built tarballs that the
upstream build server already assembled. Elpaca installs from source@comma{} which is
more flexible — pin a commit@comma{} follow a branch@comma{} patch locally — but means every
build quirk would have to be replayed on the user's machine. This library does
the replay.

@node How it works
@chapter How it works

A single hook on @samp{elpaca-recipe-functions}. For each package Elpaca resolves@comma{}
the hook branches:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Metadata-driven path.} If the package has a @samp{:make}@comma{} @samp{:doc}@comma{} or
@samp{:shell-command} entry in GNU/NonGNU ELPA's @samp{elpa-packages} file (fetched on
first use and cached locally)@comma{} synthesize exactly those commands:

@example
:shell-command CMD   -> sh -c CMD   in source dir
:make TGT            -> make TGT    in source dir
:doc "D/F.texi"      -> cd D && makeinfo F.texi -o F.info
:doc "D/F.org"       -> cd D@comma{} run Emacs batch with ox-texinfo to export
                        F.org to .texi (respecting #+export_file_name:
                        and other org directives)@comma{} then makeinfo on
                        the produced file.  Handles the many packages
                        whose :doc source is a README.org (corfu@comma{}
                        consult@comma{} vertico@comma{} etc.).
@end example

Then mirror ELPA's ``ship everything in source'' packaging rule@comma{} honoring
@samp{:lisp-dir}@comma{} @samp{:ignored-files}@comma{} and @samp{:excludes} from the same entry.

@item
@strong{Heuristic path.} For MELPA-only packages (or ELPA entries without build
fields): scan the source tree for @samp{.texi} files@comma{} keep ones that look like
standalone manuals (have @samp{@@setfilename} or start with @samp{\input texinfo})@comma{} run
@samp{makeinfo --force} on each from its own directory. @samp{@@include} fragments
(fdl.texi@comma{} gpl.texi@comma{} etc.) are detected and skipped. This is a best-effort
heuristic — no upstream metadata@comma{} no guarantees. It handles the normal
case (one @samp{.texi} manual@comma{} maybe a few fragments@comma{} no exotic build pipeline)
and quietly produces nothing for packages that don't fit. If a MELPA
package has a weirder build (texi generated by a Makefile target@comma{} texi
fetched over the network at build time@comma{} nested manuals@comma{} custom makeinfo
flags)@comma{} you'll have to write your own @samp{:build} for that one.

@item
@strong{Respect existing @samp{:build}.} Recipes that already set @samp{:build} (e.g.
@samp{elpaca-menu-org}'s custom pipeline for Org) are left alone.
@end enumerate

In both paths the library disables @samp{elpaca-build-docs} (which has multi-file
@samp{makeinfo} bugs) and queues @samp{elpaca--install-info} so the @samp{dir} entry gets
updated.

@node Companion verifying parity against @samp{packageel}
@chapter Companion: verifying parity against @samp{package.el}

A sibling file@comma{} @uref{elpaca-build-augment-audit.el, elpaca-build-augment-audit.el}@comma{} downloads the latest tarball
for each package from GNU/NonGNU ELPA or MELPA and diffs it against the
Elpaca build dir@comma{} so you can confirm — per package or across the whole
config — that nothing a @samp{package.el} user would have is missing from your
Elpaca install.  See @uref{TEST.org, TEST.org} for the full writeup and the three entry
points (@samp{M-x elpaca-build-augment-audit}@comma{} @samp{-audit-all}@comma{} and the opt-in
@samp{elpaca-build-augment-audit-watch} list).

@node Install and use
@chapter Install and use

System requirements: @samp{makeinfo} (part of texinfo)@comma{} @samp{make}@comma{} and @samp{sh} on @samp{PATH}.
On Windows: MSYS2 or Git for Windows provides all three.

The elisp block below goes @strong{below} Elpaca's own installer boilerplate — see
@uref{https://github.com/progfolio/elpaca/blob/master/doc/manual.md#installer} for
the boilerplate itself (copy it verbatim into your init before the block
below).

@lisp
;; Install elpaca-build-augment via Elpaca and enable the hook in its
;; body.  Elpaca resolves recipes synchronously at the moment each
;; `(elpaca ...)' form is evaluated -- the clone/build pipeline is
;; async@comma{} but the recipe-functions hook fires at queue time.  So the
;; hook has to be *registered* (not just textually earlier) before
;; any augmentable `(elpaca ...)' / `(use-package :ensure t)' form
;; is evaluated.  `:wait t' on the recipe tells Elpaca to install@comma{}
;; run the body (`-enable')@comma{} and only then let evaluation continue.
(elpaca (elpaca-build-augment
         :host github
         :repo "jam1015/elpaca-build-augment"
         :wait t)
  ;; Optional configuration -- all have sensible defaults@comma{} uncomment
  ;; only what you want to change.  See the "Configuration" section
  ;; below for details.
  ;;
  ;; (setopt elpaca-build-augment-sources
  ;;         elpaca-build-augment--sources-savannah)  ; default: emacsmirror
  ;; (setopt elpaca-build-augment-cache-metadata nil) ; default: t
  (elpaca-build-augment-enable)
  ;; Optional: audit builds against package.el tarballs.  See TEST.org.
  (require 'elpaca-build-augment-audit)
  (setopt elpaca-build-augment-audit-watch '(auctex ess eat))
  (elpaca-build-augment-audit-enable))

;; -- Option A: use-package integration --
;; No `:wait' needed here: Elpaca's built-in `elpaca-menu-extensions'
;; already injects `:wait t' into the recipe for elpaca-use-package.
(elpaca elpaca-use-package (elpaca-use-package-mode))

(use-package auctex :ensure t :demand t) ; `make elpa' + makeinfo
(use-package ess    :ensure t)           ; cd doc && makeinfo ess.texi
(use-package eat    :ensure t)           ; makeinfo eat.texi@comma{} skip fragments
(use-package bongo  :ensure t)           ; MELPA-only; heuristic scan finds bongo.texi

;; -- Option B: raw elpaca --
(elpaca evil)                            ; NonGNU ELPA@comma{} :doc + :ignored-files
(elpaca sicp)                            ; MELPA-only; scan fails safely on broken texi
@end lisp

On first use the library fetches GNU and NonGNU ELPA's @samp{elpa-packages} files
(by default from the emacsmirror GitHub mirrors) into
@samp{<user-emacs-directory>/elpaca-build-augment-cache/}. No metadata is bundled
with the package — the elisp does all the work of discovering per-package
build structure.

