@node Metadata source @samp{elpaca-build-augment-sources}
@section Metadata source: @samp{elpaca-build-augment-sources}

Where the @samp{elpa-packages} files come from. Two presets are shipped:

@itemize
@item
@samp{elpaca-build-augment--sources-emacsmirror} (default) — the @samp{emacsmirror}
GitHub mirrors of @samp{elpa.git} / @samp{nongnu-elpa.git}. Backed by GitHub's CDN@comma{}
fast and stable.
@item
@samp{elpaca-build-augment--sources-savannah} — the upstream Savannah cgit
endpoints. Authoritative@comma{} but cgit is frequently overloaded and returns 502
error pages under traffic bursts. Opt in if you specifically want
first-source reads.
@end itemize

You can also set the variable to an arbitrary alist to point at any other
host (a local mirror@comma{} a corporate proxy@comma{} etc.). See the @samp{defcustom} docstring
for the plist shape.

@lisp
;; Opt into Savannah upstream instead of emacsmirror.
(setopt elpaca-build-augment-sources
        elpaca-build-augment--sources-savannah)
@end lisp

@node Metadata caching @samp{elpaca-build-augment-cache-metadata}
@section Metadata caching: @samp{elpaca-build-augment-cache-metadata}

Boolean@comma{} default @samp{t}.

@itemize
@item
@samp{t} — the fetched snapshots are persisted on disk under
@samp{<user-emacs-directory>/elpaca-build-augment-cache/} and reused across
Emacs sessions. Call @samp{M-x elpaca-build-augment-refresh} to pull fresh
copies.
@item
@samp{nil} — no disk cache. The snapshots are fetched once per Emacs session
into an in-memory index and reused for every package install in that
session; @samp{M-x elpaca-build-augment-refresh} forces a refetch within a
session.
@end itemize

Disk caching is recommended for normal use — the metadata changes slowly and
a stale copy only costs you a build step or two on a newly-added field.
Choose @samp{nil} if you specifically want every Emacs session to start from fresh
upstream metadata.

@lisp
(setopt elpaca-build-augment-cache-metadata nil)
@end lisp

@node Audit watch list @samp{elpaca-build-augment-audit-watch}
@section Audit watch list: @samp{elpaca-build-augment-audit-watch}

List of package symbols that get an automatic parity report against
@samp{package.el} tarballs after Elpaca finishes building them. Only consulted
when @samp{(elpaca-build-augment-audit-enable)} has been called. See
@uref{TEST.org, TEST.org} for details.

@lisp
(setopt elpaca-build-augment-audit-watch '(auctex ess eat))
@end lisp

@node Introspection
@section Introspection

See what the library would do for a specific package:

@example
M-x elpaca-build-augment-explain RET auctex RET
@end example

Prints the ELPA entry (if any) and the exact shell commands that would
be injected.

@node Refreshing the ELPA metadata
@section Refreshing the ELPA metadata

The cached @samp{elpa-packages} snapshots are fetched on first use. To pull fresh
copies from upstream later:

@example
M-x elpaca-build-augment-refresh
@end example

@node Overriding on a per-package basis
@section Overriding on a per-package basis

Nothing library-specific is needed. The hook only ever returns @samp{:build}@comma{} and
only when the user hasn't already set it. Everything else — @samp{:branch}@comma{} @samp{:repo}@comma{}
@samp{:files}@comma{} @samp{:ignored-files} — can be adjusted inline in the @samp{(elpaca ...)} /
@samp{(use-package ... :ensure (...))} form; Elpaca's recipe merging gives the
user's declaration higher precedence than the menu's. To exempt a specific
package from augmentation entirely@comma{} just declare its own @samp{:build} yourself —
the hook sees the field is set and skips the package.

@lisp
;; Change a repo field inline — hook doesn't touch :branch@comma{} so it stays.
(use-package somepkg :ensure (:branch "release"))

;; Opt out of augmentation for one package by giving it an explicit :build.
(use-package weirdpkg :ensure (:build nil))
@end lisp

@node Limitations
@chapter Limitations

@itemize
@item
Packages whose build requires a C compiler plus system libraries (pdf-tools'
@samp{epdfinfo}@comma{} vterm's module@comma{} irony-mode's server) are installed as elisp only@comma{}
same as under vanilla Elpaca and under @samp{package.el}. You'll still need the
package's own @samp{M-x pdf-tools-install} / equivalent plus the system packages.

@item
GNU ELPA @samp{:core} packages (bind-key@comma{} eglot@comma{} eldoc@comma{} erc@comma{} …) are copies of
files inside Emacs's own source tree@comma{} not independent repos. There is nothing
for Elpaca to clone@comma{} so this library can't install them from source. You
already have them bundled with Emacs anyway; if you specifically want the
ELPA-newer version@comma{} use @samp{:type tar :inherit elpaca-menu-gnu-elpa}.
@end itemize

@node Caveat install-time filesystem scan
@chapter Caveat: install-time filesystem scan

Both paths recursively walk the package's source tree at install time — the
ELPA path to build a flat @samp{(source-file . build-file)} pair list@comma{} the heuristic
path to find @samp{.texi} manuals. The walk is cheap for normal packages (~10 ms per
install) and happens only on install/rebuild@comma{} not on every Emacs startup@comma{} but
it's unavoidable with elpaca's current internals: @samp{elpaca-generate-autoloads}
and friends skip symlinked directories@comma{} so we can't take the simpler route of
@samp{:files ("*" (:exclude ...))} and let elpaca symlink @samp{lisp/} as a whole
directory — nested @samp{.el} files would be invisible to autoload scanning. We have
to symlink files individually.

If upstream Elpaca ever grew the ability to follow directory symlinks the walks
here could be shrunk or removed entirely.

@node Regenerating the manual
@chapter Regenerating the manual

@samp{elpaca-build-augment.texi} is generated from @samp{README.org} via
@samp{ox-texinfo}. If you change the README@comma{} regenerate and commit it:

@example
make doc        # produces elpaca-build-augment.texi
make info       # optional: locally preview the .info
@end example

